# Resolution on Bus Nationalisation
Tamil title: பேருந்து நாட்டுடமை தீர்மானம்

Speech No. 37, 3 September 1959

**C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Speaker, I wish to say a few words in support of the resolution moved by the honourable Annamalai. At a time when a party claiming to have adopted socialism as its programme is in power, it is not merely strange but somewhat painful that members of the ruling party should oppose so strongly a resolution calling for the nationalisation of buses.

Those opposing the resolution ask why many other industries should not also be nationalised. That is no argument against nationalising buses. When one side proposes that a particular industry be nationalised, to say that numerous other fields also remain to be nationalised amounts to asking us to include all of them; it cannot serve as an argument for rejecting this proposal. No opponent has yet given an adequate answer to the reasons advanced by those who moved and supported the resolution. Instead, the Minister spoke like a college professor, explaining how socialism stood in the eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth centuries and asking us to read what is written in certain books.

We are not obliged to accept everything found in those books. The Minister should not imagine that no books refute them. He says socialism does not necessarily mean nationalising everything and that its doctrine has changed in this century. He argues that democratic socialism requires some property to remain privately owned and some to become government property. When I see this half-and-half socialism, like our Ardhanarishvara image with one half male and the other female, I feel not only surprise but sorrow. The ruling party may instead compare itself to a peacock's neck, showing a different colour whichever way it turns. Such clever speech may display debating skill, but it does not help us reason through the issue.

No one opposing this resolution has offered a sound reason why the bus industry should not be nationalised. Neither the Minister nor ruling-party members have said that sufficient money is unavailable. They merely proclaim that they fear no one and already know everything. Such confidence may arise from the numerical strength they possess in the name of democracy, but there is neither wisdom nor skill in that argument.

Ruling-party members also say, “We are not afraid of the bus proprietors.” Why should they be afraid when they keep the proprietors on their side and argue that nationalising buses would not be socialism? Such a declaration would have meaning, courage and wisdom only after they had adopted nationalisation as policy and begun acting upon it. Keeping the proprietors beside them and claiming fearlessness is like a showman standing with a tiger and announcing that he is unafraid because he has a gun.

Bus proprietors are becoming a new class of wealthy men. Kari Gounder may say that exemplary men exist among them, and his experience of their hospitality may have taught him so. Yet no one can deny that they accumulate great wealth. By the same reasoning, one could oppose a ceiling on land because zamindars and mirasidars build mansions, temple towers, tanks, schools and choultries. A few charitable men cannot justify concentrating wealth in one place while thousands remain poor. No one calls such a condition socialism.

Nor do we claim that every bus proprietor lacks charity and that the industry must therefore be taken from them. To say that some proprietors do good deeds and that the industry must consequently remain in their hands is meaningless.

Members offer another piece of economics: the money accumulated by bus owners has not gone to China, East Germany or West Germany; it remains here and therefore increases the nation's wealth. A thief caught in court could similarly say, “I did not send this money to Australia or America; it will remain in this country.” The court would laugh, or treat it as contempt. Numerical strength may let the ruling party ask what it matters if the money remains with those proprietors. Even a mosquito sucking a person's blood might argue that the blood has not gone to a bank and that it makes no difference whether it is in this body or another. A mosquito would not make such an argument, but the ruling party does so because of its numbers.

The Minister says the socialism we accept is not that of the eighteenth or nineteenth century, that everything may be nationalised under communism but cannot be under democracy. Yet Britain nationalised its coal mines while retaining democracy. Labour was then in office and the Conservatives opposed it, but a democratic country can acquire the public support and courage needed to nationalise public industries without violating democracy.

When the ruling party says nationalising buses violates democracy, another meaning appears. Democracy means elections; elections mean expenditure; expenditure requires someone to supply money; and money must accumulate somewhere. Today it accumulates with bus proprietors, who help the ruling party during elections. Their real argument seems to be that buses must not be nationalised if their democracy is to succeed. No political treatise establishes that this arrangement accords with democracy or socialism. Even if one did, we need not accept it; economists in those same countries have expressed contrary views.

Whether Ashoka Mehta, Masani, Rajagopalachari or anyone else says something, we should accept only what our minds judge right. The Minister cannot settle the matter by displaying a glossy book and asking us to look at it. Had he argued that there was insufficient money to introduce socialism in this field, we could have discussed when money might become available. He cannot say administrative ability is lacking, because Minister Venkataraman himself stated that workers under the Madras Government's bus undertaking suffer less than those employed by private proprietors. That demonstrates administrative competence. Why should such competence exist only in the city and not in the districts?

If the administration cannot find capable people to run a nationalised service, it can call upon Mahalingam, whom Kari Gounder offered as an exemplary figure, and ask him to operate it for the Government without allowing profit to fall. It could even prescribe punishment if he failed. My friend Haja Sheriff could likewise be given a division to run. This is what English calls “commissioning.” Just as we nationalise an industry, let us nationalise their ability. Kandasamy Mudaliar said that I too possessed ability. I thank him for acknowledging it and am ready to surrender it whenever the Government asks.

The ruling party has committed itself to a ceiling on income. Kandasamy Mudaliar seems to have forgotten this. If Congress, which bears the responsibility of governing, passes a law imposing such a ceiling, it will affect me and everyone else in society. Those with no income may rejoice. But to single me out and ask whether I will accept a ceiling on my income forgets his own party's commitment.

**M. R. Kandasamy Mudaliar:** On a point of personal explanation, Sir, I did not refer only to him. I spoke generally about the ability of story writers and included everyone. We have said that there is no money in the economy to take over and run a major industry and that compensation cannot be paid. I wish to remind the honourable member of this.

**C. N. Annadurai:** He did not name me personally, but spoke generally about placing a ceiling on the income of film story writers. Annamalai, who moved this resolution, is not a story writer. N. K. Palanisamy, who supported it, is not one. Those who opposed it are not story writers either. If a law applies an income ceiling to everyone, everyone must obey it. It is inappropriate to demand a separate bill for this purpose.

They say that lack of money prevents nationalisation. Unfortunately, the member is not a minister, and I wish to point out the difference between his argument and the Minister's. The member says there is no money. The Minister says a committee has been formed for the Third Five-Year Plan, opposition members have been invited, and those who consider the scheme important may argue there. He did not say there was no money. Whatever Kandasamy Mudaliar may think of his own responsibility for finance, the Minister bears more of it.

Lack of money may be used as a debating point, but why must money be unavailable? N. K. Palanisamy said compensation need not be paid at once: it may be issued as bonds or instalments. When zamindari estates were taken over, the full sum was not first set aside. Government ownership was declared and payment followed gradually. The same arrangement could be explained to bus proprietors. Many of them, filled with patriotism and now vigorous Congressmen, should not object to receiving compensation in specified forms and stages.

Many people speak as though they had stood beside the planners while the nation's schemes were drawn up and can therefore say, “We created them.” Many who entered Congress only recently forget what they were before those plans were framed.

The Finance Minister asked how we acquired an interest in socialism. I do not expect him to have read the constitution of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam, and he spoke so only because he had not read it. Our rules set out a Dravidian socialist republic and democracy. Socialism is part of our programme, and that is why we insist upon it here. Ruling-party members practise what may be called not *samadharmam*, socialism, but *samaya-dharmam*: whatever expediency suits each moment. Morality altered to suit the occasion is neither method nor morality.

Supporters of the resolution ask the Government to state a clear policy. Does it accept nationalisation and propose to proceed gradually, or reject it altogether? That is the issue to be decided. The profitability of bus transport is evident from those who have become bus proprietors.

Members who think cinema is profitable because they look at me can learn how profitable buses are by looking at bus owners. The farther shore always looks green. Within the last ten or twenty years a new capitalist class of bus proprietors has arisen in Tamil Nadu. In most trades goods are supplied first and payment collected later. In bus transport, a passenger pays for a ticket before the bus moves. It brings in cash every day. Other businesses operate on credit. Bus operators can buy tyres on monthly credit, petrol on longer credit and even buses on credit, while their receipts accumulate daily.

**D. Kari Gounder:** On a point of order, Sir. The member says one must pay for a ticket before boarding a bus. Story writers ask for advances one or two years beforehand. I ask him to explain this.

**Speaker:** That is not a point of order.

**C. N. Annadurai:** Kari Gounder asks for an explanation. I shall explain it when he writes a story. Bus operators can pay for their supplies by instalment while all their income accumulates every day. In a town, the local cinema theatre may soon belong to the bus proprietor, as may the rice mill and new buildings. Bus owners are becoming conspicuously wealthy, and many of the new rich are bus proprietors.

Those who framed our plans said their purpose was not merely to increase wealth but to reduce disparities in income as far as possible. Yet new hereditary classes of wealthy men continue to emerge. If that inequality is to change, nationalisation is the proper course. The Madras bus service has already demonstrated the benefits. The Government did not say then that capable administrators or compensation money were unavailable. Its present reasons make it resemble Ardhanarishvara again: capable of becoming either male or female at convenience. Such a form may have divine significance but does not suit ordinary human beings.

The Government says it possesses ability, yet fears saying that transport should be nationalised. It must have the courage either to declare that India should become a wealthy capitalist country like America, or the confidence to proceed correctly under a socialist programme. If it has the latter confidence, it must have enough concern for the public to act. Oscillating between both and calling it a “mixed economy” leaves the Government unable to administer either course effectively.

The Government takes over transport in cities such as Madras with whatever reasons suit it, but elsewhere says it will proceed gradually. It asks whether steel and iron works do not also need money. Listening to this, one might imagine that 1,008 steel plants and fifty or sixty iron works had already been established and that money was short only for additional plants. Are these plants being established in this State? Merely naming the directions in which they are being established provokes anger. Ministers from here perform penance elsewhere to obtain even a small share. Therefore lack of money is not a sufficient answer to nationalisation.

This is an empty argument fit for the eighteenth or nineteenth century, not the present. The Government's reasons resemble presenting a corpse before a court and saying that, although it now has no life, it would have given such-and-such evidence had it lived. We must attend to the underlying principles: prevent new hereditary classes of wealth, reduce economic inequality gradually, and increase government revenue. Nationalising bus transport serves all three.

Some ask why the film industry should not be nationalised. I raised that very proposal in this House last year. If you have the courage, take over the film industry; begin by nationalising theatres and increasing public revenue. Do not imagine that raising cinema will make us bow our heads. We have heard such blows for twenty or twenty-five years and stand with our heads erect. If cinema is nationalised, we are ready. Just as the talents of men such as Mahalingam could be used to operate nationalised buses, our ability could be used for a nationalised film industry.

When I said last year that I would write stories if Government took over cinema, the Chief Minister replied that writing alone would not suffice and that I must also act. I said, “So be it.” No one need think we shall escape the work of nationalisation. These irrelevant threats are like frightening a child at mealtime by pointing at the sky and inventing a monster. They will produce no result.

Kari Gounder appears to think film writers receive advances a year beforehand. Perhaps he respects me because he imagines I possess a great deal of money. In fact, the story is written, made into a film, released, and only after it has run successfully for two or three years is money paid. If Kari Gounder has written a story, I advise him not to hand it over and wander about as a debtor. Let us cease these exchanges and consider whether constructive work can be done.

Neither lack of money nor lack of ability is a sound objection. The Government itself says it has operated Madras bus transport efficiently and profitably. That experience will assist it. If nationalisation remains merely an ideal while action is indefinitely postponed, the ideal will never be reached.

The Minister says we accuse some bus proprietors of belonging to Congress. That is true. Their wealth leads them to seek protection through Congress. I do not claim that none belong to other parties. But everyone knows that if a proprietor lost all his money, no party would seek him. Our aim is not merely to detach them from Congress. It is to make them useful citizens and ordinary members of society. We do not imagine that Congress will collapse if they leave it. Nationalisation is proposed to increase public revenue and reduce the people's tax burden.

Some Congress members ask how we suddenly acquired this good intention. I cannot understand why Congressmen, who present themselves as goodness embodied and nurtured by goodwill, hesitate to support it. When one calls a dark man fair, why feel shy about calling someone as fair as Haja Sheriff fair? Their hesitation to accept the socialist objective reveals their fear of bus proprietors. I shall say it a thousand times without shame. If they claim not to fear them, let them first pass this resolution and then make that claim.

When Rajagopalachari proposed increasing tax according to the number of seats, I watched from a distance the commotion that followed in this very House. It is useless now to escape by claiming fearlessness. Bus proprietors remain with Congress because they ask what benefit its continuance in office brings them. They give Congress large sums for elections, a fact no one can deny.

The Chief Minister visited our town a month ago. The town was decorated and two arches were erected by two large bus proprietors; the name of each bus service was written on its arch. A Gandhi statue is now to be installed there, again through donations from those two proprietors, whose names will be inscribed below it. I state categorically that you refuse this resolution because you fear losing such money.

When questioned about receiving money, they cite a court decision. I draw attention to a newspaper report that a cement company gave this party Rs. 50,000 and that the court found no legal fault. They leave a large industry in the hands of ten men in socialism's name, use those men at elections to secure a majority, and then preach socialism or expedient morality elsewhere.

If those concerned about socialism support this resolution, nationalisation will suffer no harm. Passing it does not mean that every privately owned bus will enter government hands next month. Earlier resolutions show the speed with which Government acts. Acceptance would simply be an honest declaration that it accepts the objective and is bound by it.

The Minister asks that the resolution not be pressed. I ask every Congress member to use this opportunity to say, by supporting it, “We do not fear bus proprietors, and we have not abandoned socialism.” If they fail, we have nevertheless achieved the object for which we brought it: we can go to the people and say that bus transport must be nationalised. We have gained the political benefit we sought. If the Government also wishes to benefit, it can accept the resolution and announce that so many buses will be nationalised in 1961 and so many in 1962, proceeding stage by stage.

I am indebted to Annamalai for providing this opportunity. Those with purpose will use it; those seeking mere political bravado may reject the resolution. The general public is watching both choices more closely than the members of this House. With that reminder, I support Annamalai's resolution.

---NOTES---
All eighteen scans were visually reviewed and form one continuous Legislative Assembly debate with no missing or duplicated page. The printed speech number is 37 and the date is 03.09.1959; the earlier OCR read them as 87 and 08.09.1959. The English intervention “On a point of personal explanation, Sir,” was recovered from scan 7. “Nationalisation” renders both நாட்டுடமை and தேசீயமயம் according to the period usage. பாண்டுகள் and இன்ஸ்டால்மெண்ட் refer to bonds and instalment compensation. The wordplay contrasting சமதர்மம் with சமயதர்மம் is explained contextually rather than flattened.
